# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Sign-in — OpenID Connect (OAuth 2.1: authorization code + PKCE)
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The site is a *public* OAuth client: it ships to the browser, so it holds no
# secret and every one of these is compiled into the bundle. Nothing here is
# confidential — the client id is an identifier, not a credential, which is the
# whole reason PKCE exists.
#
# Any certified OpenID provider works. Free and open source, in order of how
# little you have to run yourself:
#   Zitadel    https://github.com/zitadel/zitadel      (Apache-2.0, hosted tier)
#   Keycloak   https://github.com/keycloak/keycloak    (Apache-2.0, CNCF)
#   Authentik  https://github.com/goauthentik/authentik(MIT)
#   Ory Hydra  https://github.com/ory/hydra            (Apache-2.0, OpenID certified)
#
# Register this site as a *public / SPA* client on the provider with:
#   grant types      authorization_code, refresh_token   (no implicit, no password)
#   PKCE             required, S256
#   redirect URI     https://omnidynamics.dev/auth/callback/
#                    https://omnidynamics.dev/auth/silent/
#   post-logout URI  https://omnidynamics.dev/
#   refresh tokens   rotating, one-time use
#
# Register one set per origin the build is served from — they are compared as
# exact strings. That means localhost for `npm run dev`, and the GitHub Pages
# mirror, whose paths carry the repository name:
#   http://localhost:4321/auth/callback/
#   https://<owner>.github.io/omnidynamics/auth/callback/
#
# In CI these are repository *variables*, set under Settings → Secrets and
# variables → Actions → Variables. Both workflows already read them.

# Discovery root. `/.well-known/openid-configuration` is fetched from it, so
# give the issuer exactly as the provider prints it — no trailing slash edits.
PUBLIC_OIDC_ISSUER=

# The client id from that registration.
PUBLIC_OIDC_CLIENT_ID=

# Least privilege: ask for the identity, and nothing else. Add a scope here only
# when a feature needs it, and say so on /privacy/ when you do.
PUBLIC_OIDC_SCOPE=openid profile email

# ── Choosing an upstream identity provider at the button ─────────────────────
# "Continue with Google" and "Enterprise SSO" are not separate integrations:
# Google and the customer's SAML IdP are federated *behind* the provider above,
# and the button only hints which one to jump straight to. The query parameter
# that carries the hint is not standardised, so name it for your provider:
#   Keycloak / Authentik  kc_idp_hint      Zitadel  idp_id
#   Ory Hydra             (via login UI)   Auth0    connection
#   Cognito               identity_provider
PUBLIC_OIDC_IDP_PARAM=kc_idp_hint
# The value that names each one. Leave blank and the button is not rendered.
PUBLIC_OIDC_IDP_GOOGLE=
PUBLIC_OIDC_IDP_SSO=

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Book a demo — Cal.com
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# https://github.com/calcom/cal.com (AGPL-3.0). Google Meet is not configured
# here, and is not the default: a new event type is booked on Cal Video. To put
# a Meet room on every invitation instead, connect the Google Calendar app in
# Cal.com and set the event type's location to "Google Meet" — a setting on the
# event, with nothing to change in this repository.
#
# team/slug or user/slug of the event type — the path of the booking URL with
# no leading slash, so https://cal.com/s-halim-0296y9/30min is
# `s-halim-0296y9/30min`. That link is the default in `src/lib/auth/config.ts`;
# set this only to point the site at a different event type.
PUBLIC_CAL_LINK=
# Point this at your own instance if you self-host. Defaults to cal.com.
PUBLIC_CAL_ORIGIN=https://cal.com
